NETWORK OPERATIONS AND DIAGNOSTICS (PICO W)

Inspect without changing state:
  ip                        loopback lo0, Wi-Fi wan0, RSSI
  cat /proc/net/wifi        mode, link, DHCP, IP, MAC, RSSI
  cat /proc/net/memory      lwIP memory, pbuf/PCB usage
  ping 127.0.0.1           verify local loopback
  ps                        users of process slots

127.0.0.1 is supported for local TCP/UDP tests. TCP 23 Telnet,
UDP 2000 device-control service are kernel facilities; HTTP
TCP 80 runs as user httpd. mcpd is an additional user server.
Access point SSID/hostnames depend on board Flash ID.

Use 'help nc', 'help ip', 'help ping', 'help tcpdump' to avoid
assuming Linux command flags. tcpdump and network servers can
run continuously: avoid indefinite foreground use over MCP.

Python-like socket functions:
  socket(1) [TCP], socket(2) [UDP]
  connect(fd, "127.0.0.1", port)
  listen(fd, port); accept(fd)
  bind(fd, port)  # UDP
  send(fd, "abc"), recv(fd, 32)
  sendto(fd, "abc", "127.0.0.1", port)
  x = recvfrom(fd, 32); peerport()
See /man/languages/python for data types and exact restrictions.

MCP web transport:
  mcpd --shell enables remote command execution and file write;
  no authentication/TLS. Only expose on trusted isolated LAN.
  MCP HTTP replies may be chunked. 'incomplete chunked read'
  indicates broken/incomplete response, not a Python syntax error.
  Reduce command output and inspect process/network resources.

On network failures: confirm link with ip and /proc/net/wifi,
then ping loopback and target, then check TCP/UDP server presence.
Do not change Wi-Fi configuration without authorization.
